# Gateway for HTTPS on DigitalOcean with self-signed CA

This guide walks through deploying a Twingate Gateway on DigitalOcean in front of a private Web App. The Gateway terminates TLS for the Resource using a certificate signed by a self-signed X.509 CA, then forwards each request to the upstream app with identity headers injected. The example uses [httpbin](https://github.com/mccutchen/go-httpbin) as the Web App so you can see the injected headers.

This guide highlights the key sections. A complete, runnable example with full Terraform configurations, startup scripts, and usage instructions is available in the [`examples/do-gateway-https-self-signed`](https://github.com/Twingate/terraform-provider-twingate/tree/main/examples/do-gateway-https-self-signed) directory.

~> **Warning:** This example generates private keys and certificates that are stored unencrypted in the Terraform state. Use a [remote backend with encryption](https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/settings/backends/configuration) to protect sensitive state data.

## Architecture

```
                                 ┌──────────────── Private VPC ──────────────────────┐
                                 │                                                   │
Browser ─► Twingate Client ════════► Connector ─► Gateway (:8443) ─► httpbin (:8080) │
                                 │                                                   │
                                 └───────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

The browser opens an HTTPS connection to the Gateway, using the Resource alias as the hostname. The Gateway terminates TLS using its server certificate, adds request headers, and forwards the request to httpbin over HTTP.

The setup deploys three DigitalOcean Droplets:

- The **Connector Droplet** bridges the VPC to the Twingate Client through a secure tunnel.
- The **Gateway Droplet** runs the [Twingate Gateway](https://github.com/Twingate/gateway) binary, terminates TLS, and proxies requests to the Web App.
- The **httpbin Droplet** runs httpbin in Docker. httpbin echoes back the request headers it receives.

## Before you begin

- A [Twingate](https://www.twingate.com) account with an [API key](https://docs.twingate.com/docs/api-overview) that has Read, Write, and Provision permissions.
- A DigitalOcean account with an [API token](https://docs.digitalocean.com/reference/api/create-personal-access-token/).

-> **Note:** The example uses the default VPC for your region. Droplets receive public IPs for outbound internet access, so no NAT configuration is needed. A cloud firewall blocks inbound traffic from outside the VPC.

## Setting up the providers

The `local` provider writes the CA certificate to disk so you can add it to your trust store.

```terraform
terraform {
  required_providers {
    twingate = {
      source  = "Twingate/twingate"
      version = "~> 5.2"
    }
    tls = {
      source  = "hashicorp/tls"
      version = "~> 4.0"
    }
    digitalocean = {
      source  = "digitalocean/digitalocean"
      version = "~> 2.0"
    }
    local = {
      source  = "hashicorp/local"
      version = "~> 2.0"
    }
  }
}

provider "twingate" {
  api_token = var.tg_api_token
  network   = var.tg_network
  url       = var.tg_url
}

provider "digitalocean" {
  token = var.do_token
}
```

~> For sensitive values like the API tokens, we recommend using [environment variables](https://www.terraform.io/language/values/variables#environment-variables).

## Creating the certificate authority

The Gateway serves a TLS certificate for the Resource alias. Browsers only accept it if they trust the CA that signed it, so the example creates a self-signed CA and writes its certificate to disk:

```terraform
resource "tls_private_key" "x509_ca" {
  algorithm = "RSA"
  rsa_bits  = 4096
}

resource "tls_self_signed_cert" "x509_ca" {
  private_key_pem = tls_private_key.x509_ca.private_key_pem

  subject {
    common_name = "DO HTTPS Example Twingate Gateway CA"
  }

  validity_period_hours = 8760 # 1 year
  is_ca_certificate     = true

  allowed_uses = [
    "cert_signing",
    "digital_signature",
    "key_encipherment",
  ]
}

resource "local_file" "x509_ca_cert" {
  content         = tls_self_signed_cert.x509_ca.cert_pem
  filename        = "${path.module}/do_example_https_ca.crt"
  file_permission = "0644"
}
```

The server certificate uses the Resource alias as its only DNS SAN. Use a wildcard such as `*.example.com` to cover multiple aliases without reissuing the certificate:

```terraform
resource "tls_private_key" "server" {
  algorithm = "RSA"
  rsa_bits  = 4096
}

resource "tls_cert_request" "server" {
  private_key_pem = tls_private_key.server.private_key_pem

  subject {
    common_name = "gateway"
  }

  dns_names = [var.resource_alias]
}

resource "tls_locally_signed_cert" "server" {
  cert_request_pem   = tls_cert_request.server.cert_request_pem
  ca_private_key_pem = tls_private_key.x509_ca.private_key_pem
  ca_cert_pem        = tls_self_signed_cert.x509_ca.cert_pem

  validity_period_hours = 8760 # 365 days

  allowed_uses = [
    "digital_signature",
    "key_encipherment",
    "server_auth",
  ]
}
```

## Creating the Twingate Resources

Register the CA with Twingate and create the Remote Network, Gateway, Connector, and Web App Resource:

```terraform
resource "twingate_remote_network" "main" {
  name = "demo-test-https"
}

resource "twingate_x509_certificate_authority" "tls" {
  # CA names must be unique, so the suffix lets the old and new CA coexist during
  # rotation. Since the certificate is write-only, the name change is also what
  # tells Terraform to replace this CA when the certificate changes.
  name        = "demo-gateway-x509-ca-${substr(sha256(tls_self_signed_cert.x509_ca.cert_pem), 0, 8)}"
  certificate = tls_self_signed_cert.x509_ca.cert_pem

  # The Gateway references this CA, so the API refuses to delete it while in use.
  # Create the new CA and repoint the Gateway before deleting the old one.
  lifecycle {
    create_before_destroy = true
  }
}

resource "twingate_gateway" "main" {
  remote_network_id = twingate_remote_network.main.id
  address           = "${digitalocean_droplet.gateway.ipv4_address_private}:${local.gateway_port}"
  x509_ca_id        = twingate_x509_certificate_authority.tls.id
}

resource "twingate_connector" "main" {
  remote_network_id = twingate_remote_network.main.id
  name              = "demo-connector"
}

resource "twingate_connector_tokens" "main" {
  connector_id = twingate_connector.main.id
}

data "twingate_groups" "everyone" {
  name = "Everyone"
}

resource "twingate_web_app_resource" "httpbin" {
  name              = "demo-httpbin"
  address           = digitalocean_droplet.httpbin.ipv4_address_private
  remote_network_id = twingate_remote_network.main.id
  gateway_id        = twingate_gateway.main.id
  alias             = var.resource_alias

  downstream = {
    port     = 443
    tls_mode = "TLS13"
  }

  upstream = {
    port = local.httpbin_port
  }

  request_header_rewrites = {
    "X-Twingate-User"    = "{{`{{username}}`}}",
    "X-Twingate-Groups"  = "{{`{{groups}}`}}",
    "X-Twingate-City"    = "{{`{{clientGeoCity}}`}}",
    "X-Twingate-Country" = "{{`{{clientGeoCountry}}`}}",
  }

  access_group {
    group_id = data.twingate_groups.everyone.groups[0].id
  }
}
```

`downstream` is the side the Twingate Client connects to: HTTPS on port 443 at the Resource alias. `upstream` is the port the Gateway forwards to on the httpbin Droplet. `request_header_rewrites` adds headers to every request for this Resource. The example injects a Twingate-signed JWT at the Gateway level and adds user details for the httpbin Resource only.

The example also creates a `demo-gateway-ssh` Resource that allows SSH (TCP port 22) to the Gateway Droplet's private IP, so you can read the Gateway logs through Twingate.

The `Everyone` group gives all users access to both Resources so the example works immediately. Replace it with the groups that should reach each Resource, and limit `demo-gateway-ssh` to administrators.

## Configuring the Gateway

The Gateway reads its settings from a YAML configuration file. The TLS paths are hardcoded and must match where the startup script writes those files:

```yaml
twingate:
  network: ${twingate_network}
  host: ${twingate_host}

port: ${port}
metricsPort: 9090

tls:
  certificates:
    files:
      - certificateFile: /etc/gateway/tls.crt
        privateKeyFile: /etc/gateway/tls.key

webApp:
  requestHeaders:
    # Injects into every request
    Authorization: "Bearer {{`{{jwt}}`}}"
```

`webApp.requestHeaders` adds headers to every Web App request the Gateway handles, in addition to the per-Resource `request_header_rewrites`. Here it forwards a JWT the upstream app can verify.

`templatefile()` fills in the network and port, and the result goes into the Gateway Droplet's `user_data`:

```terraform
locals {
  gateway_port = 8443

  gateway_config = templatefile("${path.module}/config.yaml.tftpl", {
    twingate_network = var.tg_network
    twingate_host    = var.tg_url
    port             = local.gateway_port
  })
}
```

## Deploying the Droplets

All three Droplets share a tag that a cloud firewall targets. The firewall allows inbound traffic only from the VPC's IP range and allows all outbound traffic:

```terraform
resource "digitalocean_tag" "vpc_only" {
  name = "demo-https-vpc-only"
}

resource "digitalocean_firewall" "vpc_only" {
  name = "demo-https-vpc-only"
  tags = [digitalocean_tag.vpc_only.id]

  inbound_rule {
    protocol         = "tcp"
    port_range       = "1-65535"
    source_addresses = [data.digitalocean_vpc.main.ip_range]
  }

  # UDP and ICMP inbound rules, plus outbound rules, omitted for brevity
}
```

Each Droplet sets `depends_on = [digitalocean_firewall.vpc_only]`. Terraform creates the firewall before any Droplet boots and destroys it only after every Droplet is gone, so httpbin is never reachable from the internet during `apply` or `destroy`.

Each Droplet configures itself at boot with cloud-init (`user_data`), rendered with `templatefile()`:

- The **Connector Droplet** receives its Connector tokens as template variables and runs the Twingate Connector setup script.
- The **Gateway Droplet** downloads the Gateway binary, writes its TLS files and config from template variables, and starts a systemd service.
- The **httpbin Droplet** uses DigitalOcean's Marketplace Docker image, so its `user_data` only starts the container:

```terraform
resource "digitalocean_droplet" "httpbin" {
  name   = "demo-httpbin"
  region = var.do_region
  size   = var.do_droplet_size
  image  = "docker-20-04"

  vpc_uuid = data.digitalocean_vpc.main.id
  tags     = [digitalocean_tag.vpc_only.id]

  user_data = <<-EOT
    #!/bin/bash
    docker run -d --restart always -p ${local.httpbin_port}:8080 mccutchen/go-httpbin
  EOT

  depends_on = [digitalocean_firewall.vpc_only]
}
```

The Gateway registers with Twingate at its private IP. Its TLS files and config are part of its `user_data`, and DigitalOcean replaces a Droplet whenever `user_data` changes. A new certificate or config therefore produces a new Gateway Droplet that boots with the new values, and Terraform updates the Gateway's address in Twingate to match.

## Trusting the CA and testing

`terraform apply` writes the CA certificate to `do_example_https_ca.crt`. Add it to your trust store. On macOS:

```bash
sudo security add-trusted-cert -d -r trustRoot -k /Library/Keychains/System.keychain do_example_https_ca.crt
```

With the Twingate Client connected, request the `/headers` endpoint:

```bash
curl https://do-nyc-http-bin.demo.int/headers
```

httpbin returns the request headers it received, including `Authorization` and the `X-Twingate-*` headers added by the Gateway.

See the [example README](https://github.com/Twingate/terraform-provider-twingate/tree/main/examples/do-gateway-https-self-signed) for Linux trust store commands, CA rotation, and troubleshooting.
